**`ember-quill`** (covers C-38bb2e3e — recover deleted/embedded files → clean directory, hard)
- Scenario: forensics on a custom "QSFS" raw snapshot image (`/app/quill.img`) — parse superblock / 128-byte inode slots / extent records, select deleted files by layered rules (deleted-flag only, CRC validation, extent/size consistency, highest-version-per-name), materialize byte-exact into `/app/recovered` (nothing extra) plus a sha256/size/version `/app/report.json`.
- Generalization: hidden images with garbage slots, stale lower versions, higher versions with bad CRC, size/extent inconsistencies, live-file decoys, empty deleted files.
- Differs from `hollow-ledge` (no WAL/XOR/password parts; different format, tool, and decoy model).

**`rust-quay`** (covers C-ce46ee62 — reverse single-key byte transform to recover valid WAL, medium)
- Scenario: SQLite WAL XOR'd with one unknown byte key on a NAS appliance; recover the key via the WAL magic (both checksum-endian variants), decode the whole file into `/app/quay.db-wal`, then extract never-checkpointed `quay_secrets` rows into `/app/secrets.json`; tool operates on temp copies so `/app` files are never checkpointed.
- Differs from `hollow-ledge` (no C program, no carving, no password reversal; two-subcommand Python tool, both magic variants, checkpoint-on-close pitfall).

